      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain quantum entanglement to me? i need to understand it for my exam but i dont really get the math part so just a simple summary would be great. also how is it different from just regular correlation?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Quantum entanglement is a phenomenon where two or more particles become connected in such a way that their properties, like spin or momentum, are correlated, regardless of the distance between them. It's like a secret handshake between the particles, where they're \"in sync\" even if they're on opposite sides of the universe. Imagine two identical, tiny, clockwork dolls, each with its own gear system. When one doll's gear is turned, the other doll's gear is instantly affected, even if they're separated by a vast distance. That's roughly the idea behind entanglement.\n\nNow, regular correlation is like having two dolls with separate gear systems, but they're not connected in any special way. When one doll's gear is turned, the other doll's gear might be affected, but it's just a coincidence. Entanglement is different because it's not just a coincidence; it's a fundamental property of the particles themselves. They're \"talking\" to each other in a way that transcends space and time.\n\nTo illustrate this, think of a pair of identical, tiny, lanterns. When one lantern is lit, the other lantern is instantly lit, even if they're separated by a vast distance. That",
